n 80/40 mmHg, tachycardia 110/min) consistent with shock. Ruptured ectopic pregnancy (B1.1) has high danger score (0.9) and matches time-critical criteria requiring immediate intervention to address potential hemoperitoneum.", "required_actions": ["Confirm pregnancy status with urinary β-hCG results", "Initiate advanced hemostatic measures if hemorrhage is suspected", "Prepare for surgical intervention if ectopic pregnancy is diagnosed"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B1: Gynecologic/Pelvic Emergency) has posterior 0.483 but contains unresolved children (B1.1-B1.3) and dangerous alternatives (B1.1 danger=0.9) remain unresolved. No termination criteria met."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.75, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3020134228187919, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'gas', 'pain'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.483}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Emergency", "posterior": 0.302, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 4, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Acute pain exacerbation during exercise is more typical of ruptured ectopic pregnancy (B1) or gallstone-related biliary…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1", "B3", "B1.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Acute Presentation vs Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, fever, jaundice\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, abnormal astrocyte morphology\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Acute Presentation vs Ruptured Ovarian Cyst with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Acute Presentation vs Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Peritonitis:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum vs Ruptured Ovarian Cyst with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal tenderness, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever, increased urinary frequency\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum vs Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Peritonitis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, dizziness, fainting, hypotension\n  Favours second: chills, fever, foul-smelling vaginal discharge, lower abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting\n\nRuptured Ovarian Cyst with Hemorrhage vs Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Peritonitis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nRelated to Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum: abdominal ectopic pregnancy, female reproductive system disease, ovarian ectopic pregnancy\n\nRelated to Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Peritonitis: abdominal and pelvic region disorder, acute female pelvic peritonitis, female reproductive system disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemoperitoneum\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Peritonitis"}
